        passageEn:
            "Aye Aye's Humanities teacher asked the class to make a short video comparing the River Nile with a river closer to home, so Aye Aye chose the Ayeyarwady, the river she had crossed by ferry on a family trip to Bagan. She began her research by learning that the Nile flooded every year in ancient times, leaving behind a layer of rich, black silt across the farmland along its banks. Egyptian farmers depended completely on this flood; without it, the desert around them could grow almost nothing, so the entire civilisation grew up hugging the narrow strip of fertile land beside the river.\n\nAs Aye Aye filmed herself pointing at a map, she explained that the pharaoh sat at the very top of Egyptian society, believed by the people to be a living god responsible for keeping the Nile's floods coming each year. Below the pharaoh came priests, nobles, scribes, craftsmen, and finally farmers and enslaved workers, forming a strict social hierarchy where everyone had their place. She also described how ancient Egyptians followed a polytheistic religion, worshipping many gods such as Ra, the sun god, and Osiris, the god of the afterlife, inside grand stone temples built along the riverbanks.\n\nFor the final part of her video, Aye Aye compared how both rivers still shape daily life today: farmers along the Ayeyarwady still plant rice in fields fed by the river's water, just as Egyptian farmers once depended on the Nile's flood. She ended her video by saying that even though thousands of years and thousands of miles separated the two rivers, both showed the same lesson — a great river can become the very heart of an entire civilisation, feeding its people, guiding its beliefs, and shaping how its society was organised.",
