        passageEn:
            "For her Humanities homework, Aye Aye, a Year 5 student at Nova School, was asked to draw a simple land-use map of the village where her grandmother lived, just outside the city.\n\nWalking along the main road with her notebook, Aye Aye noticed that most of the houses were arranged in a single long line following the road, with the river running parallel just behind the gardens. \"That's a linear settlement,\" she wrote, remembering her class notes. Further along, near the old well at the village centre, houses clustered much more tightly together -- a small nucleated cluster surrounding the well, the temple, and the market stalls.\n\nAye Aye also sketched the surrounding land use: wide rice paddies to the north, a patch of forest to the east where villagers collected firewood, and a single dirt road connecting the village to the nearest town. She realised the village had grown exactly where geography made sense -- close to water, on flat and fertile land, with a road link to the wider world.\n\nWhen she showed her finished map to her teacher, Ms. Hla Hla, she was praised for correctly labelling both settlement patterns and explaining why the village had formed in that exact location.",
