            "Nova School was holding a small exhibition on the Age of Discovery, and Aye Aye's job was to build a model caravel out of cardboard and cloth for her table. As she cut out the triangular sails, her teacher explained that this small, fast ship design let Portuguese sailors travel further than ever before, sailing even against the wind on their long voyages.\n\nNext to her model ship, Aye Aye placed two paper instruments she had made: a compass with a spinning arrow always pointing north, and an astrolabe, a round disc explorers once used to measure the angle of the stars above the horizon. She told visitors to her table that without instruments like these, sailors would have easily become lost once they were far from any coastline they recognised.\n\nFor the final part of her display, Aye Aye made three small flags labelled with the names Christopher Columbus, Vasco da Gama, and Ferdinand Magellan, and drew a simple line for each explorer's route on a world outline. She explained that Columbus sailed west across the Atlantic in 1492, Vasco da Gama found a sea path around Africa to India, and Magellan's expedition was the first to sail all the way around the globe. Standing proudly beside her model caravel, Aye Aye felt like she had truly stepped into the Age of Discovery herself.",
