            "One sunny afternoon, Moe Moe walks along the riverbank near her village with her grandmother.\n\nLooking into the clear water, Moe Moe spots several silver fish swimming smoothly, their gills opening and closing steadily as they breathe underwater.\n\nHer grandmother explains that fish cannot survive out of water for long, since their gills can only take in oxygen from water, unlike the lungs people use to breathe air.\n\nSuddenly, a bird swoops down from the sky, landing gracefully on a branch above the river, flapping its feathered wings before settling into its nest.\n\nMoe Moe's grandmother explains that this bird could never live underwater like the fish, just as the fish could never fly through the open sky like the bird. Moe Moe realizes that every animal's body is perfectly suited to its own special home, whether that home is deep in a flowing river or high up in the open air, and she feels amazed at how different creatures can share the same riverbank while living such different lives.",
