            "For their end-of-term project, the whole class set up a Class Shop, selling handmade crafts and snacks to raise money for new classroom books. Moe Moe, Nay Chi, and Zaw Zaw's stall sold friendship bracelets they had made themselves.\n\n"
            "\"Let's set a fair price,\" said Moe Moe, remembering their lessons about pricing. \"Not too expensive, but enough to cover the string we used.\" They agreed on 300 kyats per bracelet.\n\n"
            "When customers came to their stall, Zaw Zaw handled the money carefully, giving correct change every time. \"If someone pays 500 kyats for a 300-kyat bracelet, I give back 200 kyats,\" he explained to a younger student watching curiously.\n\n"
            "By the end of the day, their stall had earned quite a bit of money. \"Let's save half for the class books and use the rest for craft supplies for next term,\" suggested Nay Chi, remembering the importance of saving. Their teacher was thrilled with how well the class had applied everything they had learned about needs, wants, saving, and buying and selling in one exciting shop.",
