        titleEn: "The Robe-Weaving Race",
        titleMy: "သင်္ကန်းရက်ပြိုင်ပွဲ",
        passageEn:
            "One full moon after Thadingyut's lights had faded, Su Su's whole village gathered for Tazaungdaing, ready for a very different kind of celebration. \"Tonight, we weave,\" her grandmother said, setting up a wooden loom.\n\n"
            "Su Su watched as villagers took turns at spinning wheels and looms, working through the night to weave special robes from raw cotton. \"We have to finish before the sun rises,\" her grandmother explained, \"so the robes can be offered to the monks fresh, the very next morning.\"\n\n"
            "By midnight, Su Su's fingers ached from helping spin thread, but she refused to stop, caught up in the excitement of the village working together toward one shared goal. Elderly weavers guided the hands of children, while teenagers kept the fires burning for warmth and light.\n\n"
            "Just as the sky began to lighten, the last robe was finished, and the whole village erupted in cheers. Su Su watched proudly as the finished robes were carried to the monastery and offered to the monks. \"We did it together,\" she said sleepily to her grandmother. \"That's exactly the point,\" her grandmother replied. \"Tazaungdaing isn't about one person weaving — it's about a whole community working through the night for something meaningful.\"",
